                <pb facs="0980" n="860" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg860"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.1051/346: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Beaulac</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-01-07T16:00:00-05:45">January 7, 1930—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:11 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>3. Department’s instruction number 614, of December 27, 1929, second paragraph.
                    Do I understand correctly that the Department does not consider banditry a
                    military offense?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Beaulac</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
